Q: Is void a data type in C? A: <p>Yes <strong>void</strong> is actually a thing which exists in C language.. tbh I am also inexperienced in C but I will try my best to make it clear.</p>
<p><strong>What acutally is void?</strong>
<code>void is a type but it doesnot behave like normal types like int, char, or double</code></p>
<p><strong>What does it actually means?</strong>
<code>void basically means nothing or no data can be stored there || It doesn't have a size range so technically there are no possible values to store init</code></p>
<p>Let's see some examples and try to understand it
<strong>void return type</strong>
this funtion does something but returns nothing</p>
<pre><code>void foo() {
  // does some stuff
  // returns nothing
}
</code></pre>
<p>Calling <strong>foo</strong></p>
<pre><code>int f = foo(); 
</code></pre>
<p>Compiler error as there is no value to assign to f</p>
<p><strong>void in parameter list</strong></p>
<pre><code>int main(void) {
  // does someting
  return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>So, this function is strict as it doesnot takes any arguments</p>
<p><strong>void -- generic pointer</strong></p>
<pre><code>void *v;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>p</strong> -&gt; Can point to anyting be it int, double or even struct!</p>
<p>Note: We cannot dereference it directly as our compiler doesnot know where it actually points to.</p>
<pre><code>int i = 69;
void *v = &amp;i; // this throws an error

// we have to typecast it first
int *i_i = (int *)v; // typecast it to int *
printf(&quot;%d\n&quot;,*i_i);
</code></pre>
<p><strong>So is void a datatype?</strong>
Techically yes
From <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2310.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ISO/IEC 9899</a> (pg-45 point 19) they calls it &quot;The void type comprises an empty set of values; it is an incomplete object type that cannot be completed.&quot;
In simple terms:</p>
<ol>
<li>void is a type</li>
<li>it has zero value</li>
<li>it is incomplete forever</li>
<li>it is actually a placeholder: it tells compiler &quot;there is no actual data here&quot;</li>
</ol>

Q: better way to concatenate multiple strings in c? A: <p>There's a function for it, similar to <a href="https://pkg.go.dev/strings#example-Join" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Go's strings.Join()</a>, called <code>strjoin</code>(). It <a href="https://github.com/Projeto-Pindorama/heirloom-ng/blob/304ed79eacf7dc72531851f220d72fe03be524f9/libcommon/strjoin.c" rel="nofollow noreferrer">exists as as part of Heirloom NG's libcommon</a>, but it can be just used independently.<br />
For using it, you will just need to create an array with the strings you want to concatenate plus a <code>NULL</code> as the sentinel. It's simple and clean.<br />
Here's the code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c-like prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

char *strjoin(const char *elems[], char *sep) {
    size_t en = 0,
           i = 0,
           joinlen = 0;
    const char **elem = NULL;
    char *joinbuf = NULL,
         *joinbufp = NULL;

    for (elem = elems; *elem; elem++, en++)
        joinlen += strlen(*elem);
    joinlen += ((strlen(sep) * (en - 1)) + 1);

    /*
     * Populate the buffer.
     * There's no actual necessity for nullifying it
     * since we've already estimated its size to fit
     * the new string perfectly, ergo an unnecessary
     * operation is saved.
     */
    joinbuf = malloc((joinlen * sizeof(char)));
    joinbufp = joinbuf;

    for (i = 0; i &lt; en; i++) {
        joinbufp = stpcpy(joinbufp, elems[i]);
        if (i &lt; (en - 1))
            joinbufp = stpcpy(joinbufp, sep);
    }

    return joinbuf;
}
</code></pre>
<p>It was written by <a href="https://github.com/Projeto-Pindorama/heirloom-ng/blob/304ed79eacf7dc72531851f220d72fe03be524f9/libcommon/strjoin.c#L6-L10" rel="nofollow noreferrer">me and also had some essential adjustments by Arthur Bacci</a>, a long-time contributor for Heirloom NG and also a friend of mine.</p>
<p>Hope it's of use. :^)</p>
